{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 43, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "Raymond III served as the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187 and he held immense influence within the Kingdom of Jerusalem during a turbulent era marked by constant warfare with Muslim forces. He was the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem which gave him royal blood through his mother and this lineage helped him secure power early in his life. His reign began under difficult circumstances because his father was assassinated by the Order of Assassins so Raymond had to navigate political instability while defending his borders against Nur ad-Din.\n\n*Early Life and Succession Challenges*\nRaymond faced immediate threats upon assuming the title since the County of Tripoli remained vulnerable to external attacks and internal dissent. He managed to stabilize the region through diplomatic alliances and military fortifications that protected key coastal cities. His marriage to Eschiva of Bures brought him the Lordship of Tiberias and this acquisition expanded his territorial control significantly within the kingdom. This union also placed him at the center of court politics where he often acted as a regent or advisor to kings who were either minors or incapacitated by illness.\n\n*Role as Regent and Political Leader*\nHe served as regent for Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy and could not rule without assistance during many years of his reign. Raymond demonstrated considerable administrative skill while managing state affairs and leading armies against Saladin who was rising as a powerful adversary in the region. The count advocated for peaceful negotiations with Muslim leaders whenever possible because he understood the fragility of the Crusader states and the need to preserve resources. His cautious approach sometimes brought him into conflict with more aggressive factions like the Knights Templar or Guy of Lusignan who preferred direct军事 confrontation over diplomacy.\n\n*Legacy and Final Years*\nRaymond retired to Tripoli after the disastrous Battle of Hattin in 1187 where many Christian forces were destroyed and he died shortly thereafter later that same year. Historians view him as one of the most capable rulers of the Latin East due to his strategic mind and his ability to balance competing interests among nobles and military orders. His death marked the end of an era because no subsequent leader possessed his level of experience or political acumen during the decline of the kingdom. The loss of his guidance contributed to the rapid collapse of Crusader holdings following the victory of Saladin and the eventual fall of Jerusalem to Muslim forces. His life story illustrates the complex challenges faced by Christian rulers in the Levant during the twelfth century.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -5.710288e-05}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " served", "logprob": -0.0017609595}, {"token": " as", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " the", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -7.15256e-07}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0013540012}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.039401766}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -9.536748e-07}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -1.192093e-07}, {"token": " until", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -5.448013e-05}, {"token": " death", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -4.4107533e-06}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -6.5565323e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -5.3644326e-06}, {"token": "8", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "7", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -2.861027e-06}, {"token": " he", "logprob": -0.0005541787}, {"token": " held", "logprob": -2.9212697}, {"token": " immense", "logprob": -0.48892